╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ none                                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ The current 24-parameter ansatz massively overfits (train 0.88 vs. val 0.63 vs. test      │
│                          │ 0.56, worst-group margin only 0.03). The instructions state that fewer distinct trainable │
│                          │ parameters *always* score higher and that robust separation on the hardest validation     │
│                          │ group dominates the score. I redesign the ansatz to drastically cut the parameter count   │
│                          │ via deliberate sharing while keeping enough structural diversity to remain expressive     │
│                          │ across 3 repeated applications:                                                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ - Two single-qubit RY parameters shared across alternating (even/odd) qubits, giving each │
│                          │ half-lattice an independent local rotation while still tying 4 qubits per parameter       │
│                          │ (encourages permutation-symmetric, generalizable features rather than per-qubit           │
│                          │ overfitting).                                                                             │
│                          │ - A ring of ZZ Ising couplings with ONE shared parameter, giving nearest-neighbor         │
│                          │ entangling structure that is repeated identically at every position                       │
│                          │ (translation-invariant ansatz component, a strong inductive bias that tends to generalize │
│                          │ better with small training sets).                                                         │
│                          │ - A long-range CRZ layer connecting each qubit to its "antipodal" partner (i, i+4) with a │
│                          │ single shared parameter, injecting entanglement across the whole register beyond nearest  │
│                          │ neighbors, important since the readout observable acts on qubit pairs that may be far     │
│                          │ apart.                                                                                    │
│                          │ - A final shared RZ layer (one parameter) on all qubits, giving a global phase-shaping    │
│                          │ stage before the next repetition/measurement.                                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Total distinct circuit parameters: 5 (down from 24), plus the 2 fixed readout params      │
│                          │ (gain, bias) = 7 total trainable parameters. This should substantially reduce the         │
│                          │ generalization gap and increase parameter efficiency, while the ring + long-range         │
│                          │ entangling structure repeated 3 times (per N_REPEATS) still builds meaningful entangling  │
│                          │ depth for the two-qubit readout to exploit.                                               │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0608                                                                                   │
