│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ none                                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ The seed spec uses 24 independent parameters (one RY, one pre-RZ, one post-RZ per qubit), │
│                          │ which is far too many for a tiny training set and leads to a large train/validation/test  │
│                          │ gap (0.88/0.63/0.56) and near-zero worst-group margin. Since fewer distinct parameter     │
│                          │ names strictly improve the score, and generalization on held-out hard samples is what     │
│                          │ matters, I replace the per-qubit independent rotation layers with a parity-shared         │
│                          │ parameterization: qubits are split into "even" and "odd" roles, and every RY/RZ layer     │
│                          │ uses only two distinct angles (one for even-indexed qubits, one for odd-indexed qubits)   │
│                          │ instead of eight. I also replace the fixed CZ ring with a parametrized CRZ ring that      │
│                          │ shares a single entangling angle across all edges (plus a closing edge to make it a full  │
│                          │ ring), so the circuit can learn how much correlation to inject between neighboring qubits │
│                          │ — this is a much more targeted lever for shaping the pairwise correlators that the fixed  │
│                          │ readout measures, at the cost of only one more parameter.                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This drops the parameter count from 24 distinct names down to 5 (ry_even, ry_odd,         │
│                          │ rz_even, rz_odd, ent_theta), a >4x reduction, while retaining enough structural asymmetry │
│                          │ (even vs odd qubit roles, plus a trainable entangling ring) to shape the correlators used │
│                          │ by the readout. Fewer parameters directly reduces overfitting risk on the small training  │
│                          │ set, should shrink the train/test gap, and (per the stated scoring rule) improves the     │
│                          │ score on parameter economy grounds alone, while the tunable entangling ring gives the     │
│                          │ optimizer a direct handle on the two-qubit correlations that determine the readout —      │
│                          │ plausibly helping the worst-case validation-group margin as well.                         │
