╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ none                                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ The current ansatz uses 24 independent parameters (one RY/RZ per qubit plus a second      │
│                          │ independent RZ layer), which massively overfits on the tiny training set (train acc 0.88  │
│                          │ vs test acc 0.56, a 0.31 generalization gap) and scores poorly on parameter economy,      │
│                          │ which the metric rewards monotonically.                                                   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ I propose replacing the per-qubit independent rotation layers with heavily                │
│                          │ parameter-shared rotations (a single shared RY angle and a single shared RZ angle applied │
│                          │ identically to all 8 qubits), an entangling ring of parametrized ZZ interactions sharing  │
│                          │ a single angle, and a final shared RZ layer. This reduces the circuit's distinct          │
│                          │ trainable parameters from 24 down to just 4 (ry, rz, zz, rz2), for a total of 6           │
│                          │ parameters including gain/bias — a >4x reduction.                                         │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The rationale:                                                                            │
│                          │ 1. Since the circuit block is repeated 3 times with tied parameters, even a small number  │
│                          │ of shared angles produces a non-trivial (non-commuting, Trotter-like) effective unitary   │
│                          │ with real expressive power, while drastically cutting the parameter count that the score  │
│                          │ explicitly rewards without diminishing returns.                                           │
│                          │ 2. Full permutation-symmetric rotation layers act as a strong inductive bias /            │
│                          │ regularizer appropriate for a tiny training set, directly targeting the observed large    │
│                          │ train-test generalization gap.                                                            │
│                          │ 3. Keeping one tunable entangling parameter (shared ZZ angle around a ring) retains the   │
│                          │ ability to correlate qubits (needed for the two-qubit readout pairs) without              │
│                          │ qubit-specific overfitting, and avoids the fixed-CZ option which offers no tunability at  │
│                          │ all.                                                                                      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This trades some raw fitting capacity for much better held-out and worst-group            │
│                          │ robustness, which the scoring function weights most heavily.                              │
