│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ none                                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ The current ansatz uses 24 independent parameters (RY+RZ on every qubit plus a final RZ   │
│                          │ layer), which the metrics show is badly overfitting: train accuracy 0.88 vs validation    │
│                          │ 0.63 and test 0.56, a 0.31 generalization gap, and the score explicitly penalizes         │
│                          │ parameter count "with no point of diminishing returns." With a very small training set,   │
│                          │ this over-parameterized circuit cannot generalize.                                        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ I replace the ansatz with a much more parameter-economical, translationally-symmetric     │
│                          │ design inspired by symmetry-informed variational circuits: split the 8 qubits into        │
│                          │ even/odd sublattices (a common trick for compressing parameter count while preserving     │
│                          │ useful expressivity for pairwise correlators), use a single shared entangling parameter   │
│                          │ around a ring (translation-invariant nearest-neighbor coupling, matching the fixed        │
│                          │ two-qubit-pair readout structure), and a single shared final-phase parameter. This drops  │
│                          │ the circuit parameter count from 24 to 4 (a 6x reduction), which should:                  │
│                          │ 1. Drastically improve generalization (fewer degrees of freedom to overfit the small      │
│                          │ training set).                                                                            │
│                          │ 2. Preserve enough expressivity because the ring of ZZ couplings still creates full       │
│                          │ connectivity across repeated blocks (3 repeats of the ring is enough to correlate any     │
│                          │ pair of qubits given the small system size).                                              │
│                          │ 3. Keep the circuit shallow-ish and hardware-natural (nearest-neighbor gates only),       │
│                          │ aiding convergence speed.                                                                 │
│                          │ 4. Directly help the "worst group margin" metric since symmetric parameter tying acts as  │
│                          │ a regularizer, reducing variance across different validation difficulty groups.           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This aligns with the eval's explicit preference: fewer distinct parameter names always    │
│                          │ scores higher, and robust worst-case separation with a small training set benefits from   │
│                          │ low-capacity generalizing structures rather than a per-qubit fully independent            │
│                          │ parameterization.                                                                         │
