│ patch_description        │ Builds on the previous "parity-shared" ansatz by pushing parameter economy further while  │
│                          │ enriching the entangling structure through *multi-scale* weight sharing rather than more  │
│                          │ distinct angles. Instead of splitting rotations into even/odd families (2 rotation        │
│                          │ params) and a single nearest-neighbor entangling ring (1 param) — 5 distinct trainable    │
│                          │ circuit angles — this design uses only **3 distinct circuit parameters total**: one       │
│                          │ global RY angle applied to every qubit, one global RZ angle applied to every qubit, and   │
│                          │ one global entangling angle reused across three different entangling "shells"             │
│                          │ (nearest-neighbor distance-1 ring, distance-2 skip ring, and distance-4 long-range        │
│                          │ pairing). This mirrors a convolutional-style weight-sharing inductive bias (translation   │
│                          │ invariance across scales) known to help generalization from few examples in classical ML, │
│                          │ while giving the ansatz genuinely global connectivity (every qubit pair is within reach   │
│                          │ through some combination of shells) instead of only nearest-neighbor coupling. Because    │
│                          │ the block is repeated 3 times with tied parameters, the effective circuit still reaches   │
│                          │ meaningful depth and expressivity despite the extreme parameter economy (3 circuit params │
│                          │ + gain/bias = 5 total, down from 7), which the scoring rule rewards directly, while the   │
│                          │ richer multi-scale entangling pattern is intended to produce more robust cross-qubit      │
│                          │ correlations that generalize better to the hardest validation group than a single         │
│                          │ nearest-neighbor ring.                                                                    │
