│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ none                                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ The strongest prior variant (score 0.60) used a single shared entangling parameter        │
│                          │ "theta_ent" applied identically (as CRZ) across three connectivity shells                 │
│                          │ (nearest-neighbor, skip-2, antipodal). Since apply_ansatz_block is fixed and cannot       │
│                          │ dispatch different gates per repeat, we instead diversify the *rotation axis* of the      │
│                          │ entangler across the three shells within a single block application (which is still       │
│                          │ applied identically in every repeat): CRZ on the nearest-neighbor ring, CRX on the skip-2 │
│                          │ ring, and CRY on the antipodal pairs, all tied to the same "theta_ent" parameter. This    │
│                          │ mixes Z-, X-, and Y-type two-qubit correlations without adding any new trainable          │
│                          │ parameters (still only 3 distinct circuit params + 2 readout params = 5 total), which     │
│                          │ should let the shared angle simultaneously shape complementary correlators relevant to    │
│                          │ the unknown readout observable, improving worst-group margin and generalization while     │
│                          │ preserving the parameter economy that scored best so far. This also reverts away from the │
│                          │ current 5-distinct-param version (which underperformed the 3-param version, 0.51 vs       │
│                          │ 0.60), restoring the higher-scoring parameter budget.                                     │
