                         Patch Metadata - Gen 29/80 - Novelty: 1/2 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/2                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ revert_to_crz_shells_plus_final_ry                                                        │
│ patch_description        │ The current ANSATZ_SPEC (fully-connected ZZ entangler across all 28 pairs, still sharing  │
│                          │ one angle) scored dramatically worse (0.41) than the earlier CRZ-based 3-shell design     │
│                          │ (0.56–0.60). The extra distance-3 ZZ shell apparently pushes the shared-angle entangler   │
│                          │ into a regime that hurts trainability/generalization rather than helping it, likely       │
│                          │ because too many simultaneous IsingZZ couplings with one tied angle create a highly       │
│                          │ non-convex, poorly-conditioned loss landscape for a single scalar parameter to navigate.  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This edit reverts the entangling structure to the previously best-performing              │
│                          │ configuration: CRZ (directed, not symmetric IsingZZ) shells at ring distances 1, 2, and 4 │
│                          │ only (skipping distance 3), which was empirically the strongest scoring candidate (0.60)  │
│                          │ among the prior programs. On top of that proven backbone, it adds the recommended         │
│                          │ concluding global RY layer with a new shared parameter "theta_ry_out" after the           │
│                          │ entangling shells (applied once per block, before the block repeats). This final          │
│                          │ single-qubit rotation layer acts like a QAOA mixer stage, giving the circuit one more     │
│                          │ degree of freedom to re-orient all qubits' Bloch vectors right before the (repeated)      │
│                          │ block boundary / final readout, which can sharpen the separation margin on the hardest    │
│                          │ validation group without a large parameter cost (only +1 parameter versus the             │
│                          │ 0.60-scoring version, keeping total distinct params at 4).                                │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.1101                                                                                   │
│ error_attempt            │ None                                                                                      │
│ system_prompt_id         │ None                                                                                      │
│ model_name               │ openrouter/anthropic/claude-sonnet-5                                                      │
│ temperature              │ 1.0                                                                                       │
│ max_output_tokens        │ 16384                                                                                     │
│ diff_summary             │ added: 0; deleted: 4; modified: 36;                                                       │
