│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ mid_shell_parity_split_low_param_entangler                                                │
│ patch_description        │ The current candidate (5 distinct params: ry_even, ry_odd, zz_ring, crz_long, rz_all)     │
│                          │ scored 0.51, clearly below both prior seeds (0.59 and 0.61), and especially below the     │
│                          │ 2-parameter "theta_ry / theta_z" shared-CRZ design that scored 0.61 with the best         │
│                          │ validation margin and fastest-ish convergence. Since the task explicitly states that      │
│                          │ fewer distinct trainable parameter names always scores higher, and the recommendation     │
│                          │ calls for probing whether directional (parity-based) unsharing of the distance-2 skip     │
│                          │ shell captures anisotropies better than a fully shared skip layer, I rebuild the ansatz   │
│                          │ around the proven low-parameter global-RY / global-RZ backbone (the 0.61 seed) but        │
│                          │ replace the single shared entangling parameter for the distance-2 skip-ring with two      │
│                          │ parity-tied parameters ("theta_ent_mid_even" for skip links starting at even wires,       │
│                          │ "theta_ent_mid_odd" for those starting at odd wires), while keeping the distance-1 ring   │
│                          │ and distance-4 antipodal shells tied to the cheap "theta_z" parameter as in the strongest │
│                          │ prior design. This keeps total distinct parameters low (4: theta_ry, theta_z,             │
│                          │ theta_ent_mid_even, theta_ent_mid_odd) while testing whether the small additional degree  │
│                          │ of freedom in the mid-range shell recovers/improves worst-group margin without            │
│                          │ reintroducing the parameter bloat that hurt the current candidate.                        │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
