╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ add_rx_axis_reduce_params                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ The two prior seeds show a clear trend: the 2-distinct-parameter design (shared RY +      │
│                          │ shared RZ, with CRZ entangling shells reusing the RZ parameter) scored higher (0.61) than │
│                          │ the current 4-parameter design (0.59) that added a separate entangling parameter and a    │
│                          │ final RY_out layer. This strongly supports the stated principle that fewer distinct       │
│                          │ trainable parameters generalizes better on this small-data task.                          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ At the same time, the circuit currently only rotates around Y and Z axes for single-qubit │
│                          │ gates — there is no X-axis freedom, which limits the set of reachable single-qubit states │
│                          │ (RY then RZ can only reach a 2D great-circle slice of the Bloch sphere per qubit, since a │
│                          │ global RY followed by global RZ is equivalent to only 2 real degrees of freedom, not full │
│                          │ single-qubit unitary freedom). Introducing a shared global RX layer restores access to    │
│                          │ the third Pauli axis with a single new parameter, which can help align qubits with the    │
│                          │ fixed readout observable and improve the worst-group margin (robust separation) without   │
│                          │ any per-qubit cost.                                                                       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ To keep parameter economy foremost, I collapse the entangling parameter back into the     │
│                          │ shared theta_z (as in the best-scoring 2-param seed) and remove the final RY_out mixer    │
│                          │ layer (which added a 4th parameter without demonstrated benefit). Net effect: only 3      │
│                          │ distinct circuit parameters (theta_rx, theta_ry, theta_z) instead of 4, i.e. even fewer   │
│                          │ parameters than the current program while adding a genuinely new rotation axis, which     │
│                          │ should improve both generalization (fewer parameters) and expressivity/robustness (full   │
│                          │ 3-axis single-qubit control combined with the same rich 3-shell CRZ entangling graph).    │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
