╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ fix_mixed_axis_collapse_add_xx_long_shell                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ The current candidate shares a single angle ("theta_ent") across CRZ and CRX gates        │
│                          │ applied to the SAME wire pairs in the antipodal shell (and across different-distance      │
│                          │ rings). This causes a near-total training collapse (accuracy stuck at 0.50, no            │
│                          │ convergence) because a Z-rotation and an X-rotation tied to the identical angle on the    │
│                          │ same qubits compose into a nearly input-independent net rotation, killing the gradient    │
│                          │ signal that should come from the entangled state. This reproduces the failure mode the    │
│                          │ task's recommendation points at.                                                          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Fix: revert to the previously validated structure (global RY + global RZ, with the RZ     │
│                          │ angle reused as the CRZ angle for the two short-range shells, which scored 0.61 with only │
│                          │ 2 distinct params), and apply the specific recommended change — replace the distance-4    │
│                          │ antipodal shell with genuine long-range coupling using IsingXX gates on a NEW dedicated   │
│                          │ shared parameter "theta_xx_long" instead of reusing theta_z or mixing CRZ/CRX with the    │
│                          │ same angle. This keeps generators cleanly separated (Z-type short-range, X-type           │
│                          │ long-range) so the two shells cannot destructively interfere, while adding only one extra │
│                          │ parameter (3 distinct circuit params total, 5 with readout) to specifically target the    │
│                          │ hardest, most distance-separated correlations that the recommendation argues matter most  │
│                          │ for robust separation.                                                                    │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.1270                                                                                   │
│ error_attempt            │ None                                                                                      │
