                         Patch Metadata - Gen 44/80 - Novelty: 1/2 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/2                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ none                                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ The previous candidate (using a dedicated "theta_xx_long" parameter for the antipodal     │
│                          │ shell) regressed performance (0.55 vs 0.61 for the simpler 2-parameter design). The       │
│                          │ 2-parameter design (global RY + global RZ, all three CRZ shells sharing "theta_z") was    │
│                          │ the best-performing configuration so far, but it uses only Z-type generators for all      │
│                          │ entanglers, which may limit how well it can separate the hardest validation samples       │
│                          │ (Z-only entanglement is insensitive to certain X/Y-plane state features).                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Instead of adding a brand-new parameter for long-range coupling (which hurt               │
│                          │ generalization) or reusing "theta_z" on the same wires with an X-type gate (which the     │
│                          │ seed's own analysis found kills the gradient by composing to a near-fixed net rotation),  │
│                          │ we replace only the long-range (distance-4, antipodal) shell's CRZ gates with CRX gates   │
│                          │ that reuse the existing "theta_ry" parameter. This is the natural entangling counterpart  │
│                          │ of the RY single-qubit layer (mirroring how CRZ is the natural counterpart of the RZ      │
│                          │ layer), injects a genuinely non-commuting (X-type) long-range correlation channel, and    │
│                          │ does so without adding any new trainable parameter (still 2 distinct circuit params, 4    │
│                          │ total with gain/bias) and without the same-wire same-angle double-rotation degeneracy     │
│                          │ that broke trainability before (CRX(theta_ry) acts on different wires — the antipodal     │
│                          │ ring — than the CRZ(theta_z) short-range shells, and pairs with a genuinely different     │
│                          │ generator than the co-located RZ layer, avoiding accidental cancellation). This should    │
│                          │ improve robustness on hard validation groups while preserving the strong parameter        │
│                          │ economy of the best prior result.                                                         │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.1080                                                                                   │
│ error_attempt            │ None                                                                                      │
│ system_prompt_id         │ None                                                                                      │
│ model_name               │ openrouter/anthropic/claude-sonnet-5                                                      │
│ temperature              │ 1.0                                                                                       │
│ max_output_tokens        │ 16384                                                                                     │
│ diff_summary             │ added: 4; deleted: 0; modified: 23;                                                       │
