                         Patch Metadata - Gen 58/80 - Novelty: 1/2 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 2/2                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ none                                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ Builds on the two prior "multi-shell shared-entangler" seeds that both converged to the   │
│                          │ same 2-distinct-parameter design (theta_ry for a global RY layer, theta_z shared between  │
│                          │ a global RZ layer and three CRZ entangling shells at ring-distances 1, 2, 4). Both        │
│                          │ variants scored identically (0.61) regardless of shell ordering, suggesting the score in  │
│                          │ this regime is dominated by parameter economy rather than by which of the (already        │
│                          │ saturated ~0.90 validation accuracy) shell arrangements is used.                          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Key insight applied: since "fewer distinct trainable parameter names ALWAYS scores        │
│                          │ higher, with no point of diminishing returns," we push the reduction one step further and │
│                          │ collapse theta_ry and theta_z into a single shared angle "theta" used for every rotation  │
│                          │ in the circuit (local RY/RZ layers as well as all CRZ entanglers). We preserve the exact  │
│                          │ validated 3-shell ring topology (distance 1/2/4 — the configuration proven not to break   │
│                          │ ring symmetry, unlike the earlier even/odd anisotropic split that collapsed training) and │
│                          │ add two zero-cost enrichments that reuse the same single parameter: an interleaved local  │
│                          │ RZ layer between Shell 1 and Shell 2 (extra local/non-local mixing point, validated       │
│                          │ harmless in the current program), and a final RY "output mixer" layer just before         │
│                          │ measurement (validated harmless in a prior seed) to give the optimizer one more place to  │
│                          │ re-align qubit Bloch vectors with the fixed two-qubit readout observable. Because every   │
│                          │ gate reuses the same scalar theta, the circuit becomes a single-knob, highly nonlinear    │
│                          │ (many compounded rotations) yet trivially optimizable (effectively 1D circuit landscape + │
│                          │ gain/bias) transformation, which should converge fast, keep the training/validation gap   │
│                          │ small, and score much higher on parameter economy while retaining most of the             │
│                          │ discriminative structure that produced 0.90 validation accuracy with the 2-parameter      │
│                          │ version.                                                                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Total trainable parameters: 1 circuit angle + gain + bias = 3 (down from 4), with only 1  │
│                          │ distinct circuit parameter name (down from 2).                                            │
