                         Patch Metadata - Gen 64/80 - Novelty: 1/2 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/2                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ cross                                                                                     │
│ patch_name               │ none                                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ Crossover of the two (functionally identical) 2-parameter multi-shell CRZ ansatzes that   │
│                          │ both scored 0.61. Both prior seeds share the same backbone: a single global RY angle      │
│                          │ ("theta_ry") and a single global RZ angle ("theta_z") applied to every qubit, followed by │
│                          │ CRZ entangling shells at ring-distances 1, 2 and 4 that reuse "theta_z". That design gets │
│                          │ train=1.00/val=0.90/test=0.85 but has a sizeable generalization gap (0.15) and a          │
│                          │ comparatively low worst-group margin (0.33), suggesting the entangling layer is           │
│                          │ expressive along only the Z-generator direction and may be missing correlations that      │
│                          │ matter for the hardest validation group.                                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The crossover keeps the exact 2-distinct-parameter economy (still only theta_ry/theta_z,  │
│                          │ so parameter count and parameter-efficiency stay at their best-known value) but           │
│                          │ diversifies the *generator* of the entangling layer instead of just its range: the        │
│                          │ nearest-neighbor ring shell is duplicated with a CRY version that reuses "theta_ry" (no   │
│                          │ new parameter name), giving the circuit access to both a Z-type and a Y-type two-qubit    │
│                          │ coupling built from the same two global angles. This is a "generator interleaving" idea   │
│                          │ that mixes the seed's two independent single-qubit rotation axes into the entangling      │
│                          │ structure itself, which typically improves the richness of correlations captured near the │
│                          │ readout without adding degrees of freedom to fit (and thus without hurting the            │
│                          │ small-training-set generalization behavior that parameter economy is being rewarded for). │
│                          │ The distance-2 and distance-4 CRZ shells are kept unchanged since removing them was       │
│                          │ previously shown to hurt performance, and no final mixer layer is appended since that was │
│                          │ previously shown to interact badly with a similarly-shelled 2-parameter circuit.          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Net structural change vs. the current best (0.61) program: one additional CRY ring shell  │
│                          │ at distance 1, reusing "theta_ry", inserted right after the existing CRZ distance-1       │
│                          │ shell. This keeps circuit depth/gate growth modest (8 extra gates per block, 24 extra     │
│                          │ across the 3 repeats) while giving the ansatz a genuinely 2-axis entangling structure,    │
│                          │ which should help align the state with the fixed two-qubit readout observable more        │
│                          │ robustly across validation subgroups (targeting the worst-group margin metric that        │
│                          │ dominates the score) while preserving the 2-parameter economy and fast convergence of the │
│                          │ seed.                                                                                     │
